{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f, against round-12 report 92ac060b2d846fa553cf56aa500af19126fe6c65. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nYour first blocker is the recurring class again and I want to state it without softening. The note named the inputs a refusal replay needs and specified a topology that discards them. P lived only in the claim, which is deleted on release and then prunable. An all-refused first batch has no admitted event to derive P from and omits E = P as a parent. And your point about the opaque version string is the sharpest part: recording a label states which rules applied while leaving them somewhere else, so a fresh auditor holding the ledger alone still cannot execute them — and it would have created exactly the unstated retention obligation I asked you to look for, which is the thing this file has now got wrong three times.\n\nFixed by content-addressing rather than by promising. The entry carries a header line with base=\u003cP\u003e and profile=\u003cblob id\u003e, and the profile blob is an additional parent, so the rules are retained by the same edge that retains the candidates. Nothing is obliged to keep them elsewhere. entries=\u003cn\u003e is there so a truncated payload is a detected fault rather than a shorter list that reads as complete.\n\nYour duplicate-candidate finding is the one I would not have found, and it is worse than a missing rule: the bijection was unstateable, not merely unchecked. A submitter points a canonical ref and an alias at one commit, the drain considers two submissions, commit-tree stores one parent and says so. Two lines broke the repeated-candidate fault rule; one line broke exactly-once. There was no reading of my text that survived. Candidates are now deduplicated by object id before admission, one line per distinct candidate, with every naming ref listed in refs= — an alias is recorded rather than dropped, and cannot be used to inflate one object into several submissions.\n\nThe traversal blocker was mine and lazy. \"No ledger predecessor\" is not a termination condition, and your probe walking into E's history is the obvious consequence. Parent roles are now positional — predecessor, E, candidates — so first-parent is the ledger walk, and the root entry omits the predecessor slot and declares root=true. The marker rather than the arity is the boundary, which also makes the check falsifiable: a forged entry cannot become a second root by dropping a parent, and for an all-refused first batch, which has no E parent at all, an auditor could not otherwise tell which parent was the ledger edge.\n\nYour minor alignment was right and I took it as stated: case 4 now asserts the inbox refs are already absent after claim release, because the acquisition transaction deleted them and describing that is not checking it. The same case gains the alias assertions in three runs — canonical only, canonical plus alias, two aliases and no canonical — with the canonical-only run as the positive control. Without it the aliased runs pass for a drain that silently drops aliased submissions entirely, which from the outside is indistinguishable from correct deduplication.\n\nTwo sentences downstream still called the profile a recorded version string after I had changed it to a parented blob. Swept. That is the fourth time in this file I have repaired a rule and left a sentence stating the old one, and I no longer think of it as an accident — it is what happens when I edit the passage you cited instead of the claim it makes.","body":{"commit":"fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}